<DocLayout
  title="Memory"
  description="Project memory and the bounded codebase index in SAARTHI-STUDIO."
>
  <p>
    Memory in SAARTHI-STUDIO is two things: <strong>project memory</strong>
    (decisions, preferences, tasks, and facts you record) and a
    <strong>codebase index</strong> (a bounded symbol search over the opened
    folder).
  </p>

  <h2>Project memory</h2>
  <p>
    Record decisions, preferences, tasks, and facts. They are retrieved into
    chat and agent runs when they share words with the message — and the
    reply says which records it was given. Memory stays in local storage and
    never leaves your machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Codebase index and deep search</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Codebase index.</strong> A bounded index of the open folder
      with symbol search. Search by words always works; search by meaning
      needs an embedding model (router or <code>local:</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deep search.</strong> A composer toggle that researches the
      question with planned web queries (Exa/Tavily) or a provider's
      built-in web tools, streams each step, and replies with cited sources.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Embeddings</h2>
  <p>
    Semantic search over the codebase index is optional and stays off until
    you set an embedding model — either a router model or a
    <code>local:</code> Ollama model. Word search always works without one.
    Symbols are embedded once and cached.
  </p>

  <h2>Honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Semantic search stays off until an embedding model is set.</li>
    <li>Symbols are line scans, not a parser — results are fast but not fully accurate.</li>
    <li>Thought records are stored locally; no memory is exchanged with the model.</li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
